 quality control in an international multicentre case control study</article-title><source>Ann. Occup. Hyg.</source><year>2003</year><volume>47</volume><fpage>37</fpage><lpage>47</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12505905</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><label>17.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lindsey</surname><given-names>JK</given-names></name></person-group><source>Modelling Frequency and Count Data</source><year>1995</year><publisher-loc>Oxford, UK</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Oxford University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><label>18.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Leyland</surname><given-names>AH</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><source>Multilevel Modelling of Health Statistics</source><year>2001</year><publisher-loc>Chichester, UK</publisher-loc><publisher-name>John Wiley &#x00026; Sons Ltd</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><label>19.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Grambsch</surname><given-names>PM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Proportional hazards tests and diagnostics based on weighted residuals</article-title><source>Biometrika</source><year>1994</year><volume>81</volume><fpage>515</fpage><lpage>526</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><label>20.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rasbash</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>MLwiN version 1.10</article-title><year>2001</year><publisher-loc>London, UK</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Multilevel Models Project, Institute of Education, University of London</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><label>21.</label><citation citation-type="book"><collab>StataCorp</collab><source>Stata Statistical Sofware: Release 8</source><ye